#use(std/code)

#section{Host escapes}::

#code{#(expr)} escapes from text mode into the host language (Racket). Alone
on a line it is a statement (its value is discarded — this is how
#code{#(define …)} works); inline it is an expression whose result becomes
content. #code|{#name{…}}| inside host code re-enters text mode.

#code(lang: "plain")
	#(define price 42)
	The item costs #(price).

	#(define base 10)
	#emph
		#(define bump 5)
		total #(+ base bump)
	outer #(+ base 1)

	#(apply para (for/list ([x (quote ("a" "b" "c"))]) #emph{#(x)}))

Live:

#(define price 42)
The item costs #(price).

#(define base 10)
#emph
	#(define bump 5)
	total #(+ base bump)
outer #(+ base 1)

#(apply para (for/list ([x (quote ("a" "b" "c"))]) #emph{#(x)}))

Scoping is host scoping: #code{#(define …)} inside a block body is local to
that block — #code{bump} above is not visible at #code{outer}.

Markup re-entry: inside #code{#(expr)} and #code{(args)}, the forms
#code|{#name{body}}|, #code|{#name(args){body}}| and #code{#name(args)} read
as YAMD calls producing Content, with the same body-reader dispatch as text
mode (so #code|{#m{…}}| still hits the math parser there). References inside
a re-entry body see the surrounding host bindings — #code{#(x)} above sees
the #code{for/list} variable.

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: The parens of #code{#(...)} delimit host code: one datum inside is that expression (#code{#(price)}); several datums form the compound form (#code{#(define price 42)}). #code{#()} is an error.
	#item: Reserved-token collisions: in host code, #code{#t}, #code{#f}, #code|{#hash(…)}|, #code{#x1F}, #code{#px"…"} and friends are Racket literals, never re-entry calls — a function named like a reserved token must be called as #code{(name …)} there. In #emph{text} mode #code{#hash(…)} would be an ordinary YAMD call; the asymmetry is deliberate.
	#item: #code{#(…)} nested inside host code is a Racket #emph{vector literal} — you are already in host mode, there is nothing to escape from.
	#item: Bare #code{#name} (no brace, no paren) in host code is not re-entry; it falls through to Racket's reader.
	#item: Line-oriented bodies (#code{: rest-of-line}, #code{::}) do not exist in host mode — there is no "rest of line" inside an s-expression. #code{#use} is also rejected there.
